<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danzantes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500–200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Los Danzantes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danzantes, 'dancers', are some three hundred carved stone slabs at Monte Albán in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, made in the city's earliest period, about 500 to 200 BCE. They show naked men in contorted poses, once thought to be dancers but now generally interpreted as slain or sacrificed captives, some with glyphs that are among the earliest evidence of Zapotec writing. They were set into the wall of a building on the Main Plaza.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danzantes.jpg" alt="Danzantes" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gamaliel2412 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traditional_Restaurants_and_gastronomy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
